    "framing": "Econometrics A researcher estimates y it = ρ y i,t−1 + α i + ε it using a panel with many firms but only six annual observations per firm. Which statements about estimation are correct? Dynamic panels Nickell bias Difference GMM (A), (C), and (D) are correct. In a dynamic fixed-effects model, demeaning makes y i,t−1 −ȳ i,−1 correlated with ε it −ε̄ i , producing Nickell bias of order 1/ T . Increasing N with fixed T does not remove that bias, so (B) is false. Differencing eliminates α i ; if the level errors are not serially correlated, levels dated t −2 and earlier can be valid instruments for Δ y i,t−1 . Residual serial-correlation tests help assess those moment conditions: first-order correlation in differences is expected, whereas second-order correlation threatens the usual instruments. Microeconomics A risk-averse household has utility u ( w ) = √ w , initial wealth 100, and a 25% probability of losing 64. An insurer offers full insurance at premium 16. Ignoring administrative costs, which statement is correct? Expected utility Actuarially fair insurance Risk aversion (B) is correct. The expected loss is 0.25(64) = 16, so the premium is actuarially fair and insured wealth is 100 − 16 = 84 in either state. Without insurance, expected wealth is also 84, but expected utility is 0.75√100 + 0.25√36 = 9, while √84 ≈ 9.165. Strict concavity therefore makes full insurance strictly preferred. (A) is false because the premium equals, rather than exceeds, expected loss. (C) reverses the expected-utility comparison, and (D) reverses the implication of concavity. Macroeconomics Inflation follows a New Keynesian Phillips curve π t = βE t π t+1 + κ x t + u t , where x t is the output gap and u t is a cost-push shock. Which statements are defensible? New Keynesian Phillips curve Cost-push shocks Expectations management (A), (B), and (D) are correct. A positive u t raises inflation at a given gap, so offsetting it requires a more negative gap, generating a stabilization tradeoff. Lower credible E t π t+1 reduces current inflation through forward-looking price setting. Holding expectations and u t fixed, κ maps the output gap into inflation. (C) is false: with x t = 0, current inflation still equals βE t π t+1 + u t . Environmental economics Two neighboring jurisdictions independently choose pollution abatement. Each receives only $30 of local benefit from one more unit of its own abatement, while that unit also creates $20 of benefit across the border. Marginal abatement cost is $40 in each jurisdiction at the decentralized choice under consideration. What is the immediate implication? Transboundary pollution Cross-border externalities Cooperative environmental policy (C) is correct. The jurisdiction compares its local marginal benefit, $30, with marginal cost, $40, so it will not undertake the extra unit. Joint welfare counts both the local and cross-border benefits: $30 + $20 = $50, which exceeds the $40 cost. Decentralized abatement is therefore too low at this margin. (A) reverses the direction of the wedge, (B) treats a real external benefit as if it vanished, and (D) is false because cooperation can internalize cross-border benefits even with identical costs. Causal inference A city introduces congestion pricing on 1 July. Monthly traffic has a declining pre-policy trend, drivers begin changing routes in May after the plan is announced, and a nearby untreated city has comparable data. Which practices strengthen an interrupted-time-series analysis? Interrupted time series Policy anticipation Comparative time series (A), (B), and (C) are correct. A segmented time-series model should account for the existing decline so that a July level or slope change is not confused with trend continuation. Because announcement can alter behavior before formal implementation, May and June may be anticipatory treatment periods. A credible comparison series can help absorb common shocks, but only after checking comparability and spillovers. (D) is false: a small standard error does not rule out a coincident event, misspecified seasonality, or an incorrect counterfactual trend. Statistics & inference A posterior predictive p -value is computed by comparing a discrepancy in replicated data with the same discrepancy in the observed data, averaging over the posterior. Under the fitted model it must be exactly Uniform(0,1), so the usual frequentist 5% cutoff has exact repeated-sampling size. True or false? Posterior predictive checks Model criticism Bayesian p-values False. Posterior predictive p -values generally are not exactly uniform under the null. The observed data influence both the posterior distribution used to generate replications and the observed discrepancy, often making the diagnostic conservative and concentrated toward 0.5. They are valuable for locating model–data mismatch, but a 0.05 threshold does not automatically have exact frequentist size. Exact or asymptotic calibration requires additional construction and analysis. Resource economics A resource-rich government receives a temporary oil-revenue windfall while commodity prices are volatile and domestic public-investment capacity is limited. Which policies are consistent with a permanent-income approach to managing the windfall? Permanent-income benchmark Sovereign wealth funds Fiscal procyclicality (A), (B), and (D) are correct. A permanent-income benchmark ties ongoing spending to the annuity value of comprehensive wealth, preventing a temporary receipts boom from being mistaken for permanent fiscal capacity. A rules-based fund can delink expenditure from volatile current revenue and reduce procyclicality. Where domestic capital is scarce, investment can justify departing from a pure foreign-saving rule, but absorptive capacity and project returns constrain the pace. (C) is false because a temporary price windfall does not permanently raise the resource stock or recurring revenue. Empirical research design A correspondence audit study sends two otherwise equivalent job applications to each vacancy, randomly assigning a group-signaling name within vacancy. Which design and interpretation claims are sound? Audit studies Matched randomization Construct validity (A), (B), and (C) are correct. Within-vacancy pairing blocks on job and employer conditions; paired or randomization-based inference should preserve that design. Balancing templates and order prevents those features from confounding the randomized signal. The estimand is the causal effect of the name cue on callbacks in the sampled application environment, not a decomposition of employer motives or an effect on ultimate hiring. (D) is false: repeated applications can create detection, congestion or comparison effects, so interference and research burden require design limits and ethical review.",
